```text
List, Set, and Map are three major abstractions in the Java Collections Framework, but they solve different problems.

List represents an ordered collection of elements. It allows duplicates and supports positional or index-based access. Common implementations are ArrayList and LinkedList.

Set represents a collection of unique elements. It does not allow duplicates. The ordering depends on the implementation. For example, HashSet does not guarantee iteration order, LinkedHashSet maintains insertion order, and TreeSet maintains sorted order.

Map represents key-value associations. Each key must be unique, but multiple keys can have the same value. Common implementations include HashMap, LinkedHashMap, and TreeMap.

The key difference is:

List → ordered elements, duplicates allowed
Set → unique elements, duplicates not allowed
Map → key-value pairs, unique keys

Map does not extend Collection because Collection represents a group of individual elements, whereas Map represents relationships between keys and values.

For example, a List can be thought of as:

[A, B, C, A]

A Set would be:

[A, B, C]

And a Map would be:

{101 → "Akshay", 102 → "John"}

There is also a structural reason. Collection defines operations such as add(), remove(), and contains() that operate on individual elements. Map instead provides operations such as put(key, value), get(key), and containsKey(key), which operate on key-value mappings.

Therefore, Map is intentionally a separate hierarchy in Java rather than a subtype of Collection.

In an interview, I would summarize it as:

"List is used when I need an ordered collection and duplicates are allowed. Set is used when I need unique elements. Map is used when I need key-value associations with unique keys. Map does not extend Collection because Collection represents individual elements, while Map represents mappings between keys and values, so their fundamental APIs and abstractions are different."
