Q1. What are the four pillars of OOP in Java? Explain each with a practical example.


The four pillars of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) in Java are:

Encapsulation
Inheritance
Polymorphism
Abstraction


1. Encapsulation


Encapsulation is the practice of bundling data and the methods that operate on that data within a class and 
restricting direct access to the internal state. In Java, this is commonly achieved using private fields with controlled access through methods.

class BankAccount {

    private double balance;

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

BankAccount account = new BankAccount();

account.deposit(5000);

System.out.println(account.getBalance());

2. Inheritance

Inheritance allows a class to acquire fields and behavior from another class,
enabling code reuse and specialization. In Java, it is implemented using extends.
For example, Developer can extend Employee and inherit common employee behavior while adding developer-specific functionality."


class Employee {

    String name;
    int employeeId;

    void work() {
        System.out.println("Employee is working");
    }
}

class Developer extends Employee {

    void writeCode() {
        System.out.println("Writing Java code");
    }
}

class Manager extends Employee {

    void conductMeeting() {
        System.out.println("Conducting meeting");
    }
}


