Q3. What is encapsulation? Why is making fields private and exposing controlled access through methods considered good design? Can you demonstrate it with code?



Encapsulation is the OOP principle of bundling an object's data and the behavior that operates on that data together,
while restricting direct access to the object's internal state.

In Java, we commonly achieve this by making fields private and exposing controlled access through methods such as getters, setters, or domain-specific methods.


public class BankAccount {

    private double balance;

    public BankAccount(double initialBalance) {
        if (initialBalance < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Initial balance cannot be negative");
        }

        this.balance = initialBalance;
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Deposit must be positive");
        }

        balance += amount;
    }

    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Withdrawal must be positive");
        }

        if (amount > balance) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Insufficient balance");
        }

        balance -= amount;
    }
}


