Q4. Difference between Abstract class and interface. And when would you prefer one over another

An abstract class is useful when I want to provide a common base for closely related classes, including shared state, 
constructors, and common implementation, while leaving some behavior abstract. 
An interface is primarily a contract or capability that different classes can implement, without requiring them to share the same class hierarchy.

For example, I might use an abstract Payment class if different payment types share fields like
amount and common validation logic. I'd use a PaymentProcessor interface when I want different
implementations such as Stripe, Razorpay, or another provider to follow the same contract.

The key distinction is that abstract classes are suited for shared identity, state, and implementation,
while interfaces are suited for contracts, capabilities, and loose coupling. Since Java supports multiple
interfaces but only single class inheritance, interfaces are often preferred when designing flexible enterprise systems.

I would choose an abstract class when the classes have a strong 'is-a' relationship and need to share common state or implementation. For example, 
different types of payments may share amount, validation logic, and common processing behavior."

"I would choose an interface when I mainly want to define a contract or capability that can be implemented by potentially unrelated classes. 
Interfaces are also preferable when I want a class to support multiple capabilities or when I want to program against an abstraction and easily substitute implementations.





