2. When would I add an abstract class?

Suppose we discover that all payment methods have common behavior.

For example:

validate amount
generate transaction ID
log transaction
maintain payment status

Then an abstract class becomes useful.


public abstract class Payment {

    protected String transactionId;
    protected double amount;

    public Payment(double amount) {
        this.amount = amount;
        this.transactionId = generateTransactionId();
    }

    protected void validateAmount() {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
        }
    }

    protected String generateTransactionId() {
        return "TXN-" + System.currentTimeMillis();
    }

    public abstract void pay();
}


public class CreditCardPayment extends Payment {

    public CreditCardPayment(double amount) {
        super(amount);
    }

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        validateAmount();

        System.out.println(
            "Processing Credit Card payment: " + amount
        );
    }
}



public class UPIPayment extends Payment {

    public UPIPayment(double amount) {
        super(amount);
    }

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        validateAmount();

        System.out.println(
            "Processing UPI payment: " + amount
        );
    }
}


I would start with an interface because the core requirement is that every payment implementation must provide pay(). 
CreditCardPayment, UPIPayment, and NetBankingPayment can implement the same Payment contract, and the payment service
can depend on the Payment interface rather than a concrete implementation. This gives us polymorphism and loose coupling.

If the different payment implementations also share common state or behavior, such as transaction ID generation, 
amount validation, logging, or common payment lifecycle logic, I would introduce an abstract BasePayment class that 
implements the Payment interface. The concrete payment classes would then extend BasePayment.

So I wouldn't choose an abstract class simply because the classes are related. I'd use an interface for the contract 
and an abstract class only when there is meaningful shared state or implementation.


