Q . Explain private, default, protected, and public in Java. Which one would you normally use for class fields and why?


Java has four access levels: private, package-private or default, protected, and public. 
Private is accessible only within the same class. Default is accessible within the same package. 
Protected is accessible within the same package and by subclasses, including subclasses in other packages subject to Java's protected access rules. 
Public is accessible from anywhere.

For class fields, 
I would normally use private because fields represent an object's internal state. 
Keeping them private supports encapsulation and prevents other classes from directly modifying the object's state. 
If access is required, I expose a controlled method such as a getter or, preferably when there's business logic involved, a domain-specific method like deposit() or withdraw().

I would use protected only when there is a deliberate inheritance-based reason for subclasses to access something, 
default access when package-level visibility is intentionally part of the design, and public when the member is genuinely part of the external API.






