Q . Can a subclass access a private member of its parent class? What about a protected member? Explain what happens across different packages.

A subclass cannot directly access a private member of its parent because private members are accessible 
only within the class where they are declared. The subclass can access that state indirectly through an inherited public or protected method if the parent exposes one.

A protected member can be accessed by subclasses. It is also accessible to classes in the same package.
If the subclass is in a different package, it can still access the protected member through inheritance,
but an unrelated class in that package cannot access it. Also, the subclass cannot simply access the protected 
member through an arbitrary Parent object reference from another package.

