Q 1. What is the difference between a process and a thread? Why would you use multiple threads in a backend application?


A process is an independent program in execution with its own memory space and resources, 
whereas a thread is a lightweight unit of execution within a process. 
Threads within the same process share resources such as heap memory, while each thread has its own stack and execution context.

Processes are more isolated from each other, whereas threads are cheaper to create and have lower context-switching overhead, 
but shared memory also means threads require proper synchronization to avoid race conditions.

In a backend application, I would use multiple threads when tasks can execute concurrently—for example, 
handling multiple HTTP requests, performing database or network I/O, processing background jobs, 
or executing independent computations. This allows the application to make better use of CPU resources and avoid blocking one request while another task is waiting for I/O.

For example, in a web server, multiple threads can process multiple client requests concurrently instead of forcing each request to wait for the previous request to complete.

In short: process = independent execution environment; thread = execution unit within a process. 
Multiple threads improve concurrency and responsiveness, especially for I/O-bound backend workloads.

